@page "/docs"
@layout DocsLayout
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Docs — AgentBlazor</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-article">
    <section class="docs-hero">
        <p class="docs-hero__eyebrow">Documentation</p>
        <h1 class="docs-hero__title">Accurate setup and authoring guidance for real Blazor apps.</h1>
        <p class="docs-hero__lede">
            AgentBlazor is a Blazor-native workflow runtime with in-app chat surfaces, deterministic component actions, approvals,
            and route-scoped workflow registration. The validated provider path today is OpenAI via <code>options.UseOpenAI(...)</code>.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-actions">
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Href="/docs/getting-started">Getting Started</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/docs/cli">Open CLI Guide</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/demo/workflows/response-orchestration?reset=true">Open Live Demo</MudButton>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-chip-row">
            <span>OpenAI is the most validated provider path</span>
            <span>CLI is preview-first before apply</span>
            <span>Hosted WebAssembly server+client scaffold is supported</span>
            <span>Advanced hosts stay review-first when patching is risky</span>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-kpi-grid">
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>5-step flow</strong>
                <span><code>init</code> -> <code>scaffold</code> -> <code>doctor</code> -> <code>validate</code></span>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>3 provider flags</strong>
                <span><code>openai</code>, <code>azure-openai</code>, and <code>ollama</code></span>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>Review-first hosts</strong>
                <span>Legacy server, hosted WebAssembly variants, and other recognizable nonstandard Blazor hosts</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Recommended run order</h2>
                <p>The CLI onboarding flow is explicit and now matches the repository docs.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet tool install --global AgentBlazor.Cli --version 0.1.0-preview.8
agentblazor init ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --approve
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>What still stays manual:</strong> provider secrets, environment-specific configuration values, and host-specific wiring
            on advanced Blazor hosts where automatic patching would be unsafe.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Choose the right path</h2>
                <p>The docs are split by install flow and host shape, not by demo copy.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Manual runtime install</strong>
                <p>Use the package, runtime registration, host shell assets, endpoint mapping, and one workflow capability when you want to wire the app by hand.</p>
                <small>Start on the Getting Started page.</small>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>CLI-assisted install</strong>
                <p>Use preview-first scaffold for the fastest baseline setup, then confirm the result with <code>doctor</code> and <code>validate</code>.</p>
                <small>Best fit for standard Blazor hosts and the standard hosted WebAssembly server+client shape.</small>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Advanced review-first install</strong>
                <p>Recognizable nonstandard hosts still get safe additions and targeted manual-review guidance instead of blind file edits.</p>
                <small>Examples include legacy <code>_Host.cshtml</code> server apps and Oqtane-style hosts.</small>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Current product status</h2>
                <p>The documentation site should make the current product boundary obvious.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Ready now</strong>
                <p>Workflow-first runtime, built-in chat surfaces, deterministic wrappers, preview-first CLI flow, and the featured live workflow routes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Validated first</strong>
                <p>OpenAI on a standard Blazor host is the primary production path. Hosted WebAssembly server+client scaffold is also now supported.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Still conservative</strong>
                <p>Advanced hosts remain review-first when automatic startup, shell, or routing edits would be brittle or unsafe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Paid value</strong>
                <p>Analytics, audit, durable history, and smarter guidance over time, not gating baseline component actions.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Docs map</h2>
                <p>These pages now track the current repo guidance instead of a demo-only setup story.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-link-grid">
            <a href="/docs/getting-started" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Getting Started</strong>
                <p>Manual install, service registration, host shell wiring, chat surfaces, and verification.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/cli" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>CLI Guide</strong>
                <p>Command order, provider flags, preview/apply flow, and install verification.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/hosting-models" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Hosting Models</strong>
                <p>Standard Blazor Web App, hosted WebAssembly server+client, and review-first advanced hosts.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/workflows" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Workflows</strong>
                <p><code>AddWorkflow&lt;T&gt;()</code>, route scoping, approvals, and capability authoring.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/components" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Components</strong>
                <p>Chat surfaces plus the deterministic UI wrappers available in the runtime and demo explorer.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/demo-tour" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Demo Tour</strong>
                <p>The current live routes, why the funnel is intentionally narrow, and where each proof belongs.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/troubleshooting" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Troubleshooting</strong>
                <p>What to check when the agent does not respond, actions do not appear, or scaffold stops early.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/pricing" class="docs-link-card">
                <strong>Pricing & Tiers</strong>
                <p>The current free, Pro, and Enterprise boundary and where the paid value actually lives.</p>
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
